```c
if (pru->data->is_k3 && is_iram) {
    ret = pru_rproc_memcpy(ptr, elf_data + phdr->p_offset, filesz);
}
```
```c
memcpy(ptr, elf_data + phdr->p_offset, filesz);
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because the custom `pru_rproc_memcpy` function is only invoked when the segment is in IRAM (`is_iram` is true). This limitation leads to the use of the standard `memcpy` function for Data RAM segments, which can cause unaligned access issues on the 32-bit PRU processors, resulting in kernel crashes.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if (pru->data->is_k3) {
    ret = pru_rproc_memcpy(ptr, elf_data + phdr->p_offset, filesz);
}
```
Reason:
The fix removes the `is_iram` condition, allowing the custom `pru_rproc_memcpy` function to be used for all memory segments on K3 SoCs, including Data RAM. This change ensures that the firmware loading process handles unaligned accesses correctly, preventing kernel crashes.

